   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the construction\nand financing of facilities by the dormitory authority for the New York\nAlliance for Inclusion and Innovation; and to amend chapter 371 of the laws of\n1998, amending the public authorities law relating to the construction and\nfinancing of facilities by the dormitory authority for the New York State\nRehabilitation Association, in relation to extending the expiration and repeal\nof such provisions",
    "basePrintNo": "S8815",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8815-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1676 & 1680, Pub Auth L; amd §5, Chap 371 of 1998",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8815\n\nSPONSOR: ORTT\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to the construction and financing of facilities by the dormito-\nry authority for the New York Alliance for Inclusion and Innovation; and\nto amend chapter 371 of the laws of 1998, amending the public authori-\nties law relating to the construction and financing of facilities by the\ndormitory authority for the New York State Rehabilitation Association,\nin relation to extending the expiration and repeal of such provisions\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would extend for an additional five years the authorization\nfor the New York State Rehabilitation Association, and its successor\norganization upon the completion of a merger, to receive construction\nmanagement and financial services from the Dormitory Authority. The New\nYork State Rehabilitation Association's current authority expires on\nDecember 31, 2018, and this legislation would extend it until December\n31, 2023.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend public authorities law and chapter 371 of the laws\nof 1998 to change the effective date so that the New York State Rehabil-\nitation Association, and its successor organization, the New York Alli-\nance for Inclusion and Innovation, continues to have authority to avail\nitself of Dormitory Authority Construction and management financial\nservices for another five years.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe New York State Rehabilitation Association is a private, not-for-pro-\nfit statewide organization providing leadership and advocacy on multiple\nmatters of public policy on behalf of disabilities providers and the\nindividuals they serve; it informs, educates, and supports association\nmembers in their effort to offer a diverse spectrum of services; and\nhelps integrate people with differing abilities into the community.Since\nthe New York State Rehabilitation Association's authority to utilize\nDormitory Authority construction and management services was estab-\nlished, the New York State Rehabilitation Association has had the oppor-\ntunity to offer bonds involving improvement projects and loans through-\nout the state which yield a savings in public funds.\n\nExpiration of the New York State Rehabilitation Association's authority\nwould harm significant bond initiatives underway, resulting in lost\nsavings to the state and lost funding to reinvest in critical supports\nand services to people with disabilities. Future bond issues contingent\non this legislation are expected to continue to benefit the taxpayers of\nNew York and people served by New York State Rehabilitation Association,\nand its successor association member agencies.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:  Undetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately, provided that the amendments to\nthe undesignated paragraph of paragraph (b) of subdivision 2 of section\n1676 of the public authorities law, made by section one of this act, and\nthe amendments to the two undesignated paragraphs of subdivision 1 of\nsection 1680 of the public authorities law, made by section two of this\nact, shall not affect the expiration and repeal of such undesignated\nparagraphs pursuant to section 5 of chapter 371 of the laws of 1998, as\namended, and shall expire and be deemed repealed therewith.\n",
